						<h2
							id="scenario-heading"
							class="type-subheading text-center text-(--ink)"
						>
							A scenario.
						</h2>

						<p class="mt-12">
							Let's imagine that the internet gets censored, and an anonymous developer builds chatting using
							Bluetooth.
						</p>

						<div
							class="mt-7 grid min-h-80 place-items-center rounded-lg border border-dashed border-(--line-strong) bg-(--paper) p-6 text-center shadow-[0_1.2rem_2.4rem_var(--page-shadow)]"
						>
							<p class="type-body font-medium! text-(--muted)">
								[developer sitting in the window programming]
							</p>
						</div>

						<p class="mt-7">
							You can relay messages between people, and share what you're thinking about in your
							neighbourhood, and they spread around the world.
						</p>

						<div
							class="mt-7 grid min-h-80 place-items-center rounded-lg border border-dashed border-(--line-strong) bg-(--paper) p-6 text-center shadow-[0_1.2rem_2.4rem_var(--page-shadow)]"
						>
							<p class="type-body font-medium! text-(--muted)">
								[how the app works and messaging around the world]
							</p>
						</div>

						<p class="mt-7">
							Google has to comply with the government, and takes down the app; they also give the government
							the developers real name and address.
						</p>

						<div
							class="mt-7 grid min-h-80 place-items-center rounded-lg border border-dashed border-(--line-strong) bg-(--paper) p-6 text-center shadow-[0_1.2rem_2.4rem_var(--page-shadow)]"
						>
							<p class="type-body font-medium! text-(--muted)">
								[developer gets a knock on the door from people in suits]
							</p>
						</div>

						<p class="mt-7">
							Or, due to the threat alone, you can't even dream to build it in the first place.
						</p>

						<p class="mt-7">
							Unless we do something, the first step towards that future is in
							<span
								class="font-semibold tabular-nums text-(--ink)"
								data-days-until-september
							>
								114</span
							>
							<span class="font-semibold text-(--ink)">days</span>.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							You can actually do this btw, Bluetooth reaches surprisingly far<sup class="footnote-ref"
								><a href="#fn-bluetooth-range">5</a></sup
							>
							and with enough people having networks on their phones, plus people setting up large scale relay
							stations, you could relay messages between people or around the world, all without the internet.
						</p>
						<ol class="footnotes mt-8">
							<li id="fn-bluetooth-range">
								<span class="footnote-number">[5]</span>
								around 100m.
							</li>
						</ol>
						<p class="mt-16">
							Again – we're not against Google either, more so the myth of this false tradeoff:
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5 pl-8 italic">“You can either have safety, or you can have privacy.”</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							Though that isn't true, because
							<strong><em>safety and privacy support each other.</em></strong>
						</p>
						<p class="mt-16">
							Central control of an 'app store' means it's easy to erase protests, or apps that help people
							bypass takedowns or censorship, since they can be even be erased from millions of devices in a
							heartbeat when a government says they're 'disruptive'.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-16">What might happen next?</p>

						<h2 class="type-subheading mt-28 text-center text-(--ink)">How might Google respond?</h2>
						<p class="mt-12">we can expect that google will suddenly freak and think 'oh no whato no'</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							and suddenly the problem would seem more difficult. there are multiple reasons that might not
							work and see oh no we can't do this. we um!! we just can't okay??
						</p>
						<p class="mt-16">
							there are so many vendors. and many phone firmwares and everything you know, and we know you
							have to get the code from the bootloader and an enthusiast thing with jailbreaking (distant
							memory). and we can't open that because of hackers.
						</p>
						<p class="type-subheading mt-16 pl-8 text-(--ink)">but,</p>
						<p class="mt-4 pl-8">
							it's better for everyone, including Google, if there's no scamming on Android.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-16">also, Google isn't a cartoon-ish villain; Google is a giant company with:</p>
						<ul class="mt-5 ml-16 grid list-disc gap-1">
							<li>legal obligations</li>
							<li>security teams</li>
							<li>app store incentives</li>
							<li>government relationships</li>
							<li>advertisers</li>
							<li>shareholders</li>
							<li>platform risk</li>
							<li>public relations risks,</li>
							<li>and billions of users.</li>
						</ul>
						<p class="mt-16">
							Google has real reasons to care about scams; Google also has incentives to centralise control –
							and both of these things can be true.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							So, thank you for google to letting us suggest feedback. but I'm afraid it's not enoguh..
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							We reckon that there are better options that keep people safe, and keeps a person's phone
							theirs.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-20">Surely, we can make a phone secure, and focused on the people who use it.</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							You bought it, and so it should be yours forever – since everything else is code.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							From your fridge to your phone to your tablet to your tell-y to your digital assistant to your
							cat or dog, the joy from owning your own computer comes from when it respects your rights.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-20">
							It's a little corny saying this, but at heart, it's about choosing devices that choose you.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">
							It means a couple of years down the line, when you're switching devices, thinking different –
							and wondering about phones you can repair, with little things, like removable batteries
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5">and maybe even thinking about making your own 'cyberdeck'</p>
					</section>

					<CyberdeckScrapbook />

					<section class="type-body mb-16 text-(--ink)">
						<p>
							Our devices are really powerful, and we can use them in ways that show how flexible and creative
							the people that use them are, too.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-6">
							We could do a lot of cool stuff together if everyone had a computer in their pocket. <br />We
							can eliminate scams and make a more accessible world for everyone.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-6">
							Since this isn't just for android either, but it's a pretty cool thing to start with.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-24">Your phone now has the same speed and power as your computer.</p>
						<p class="mt-6">
							A company putting digital locks on something doesn't make it more secure, it only makes the
							world less fun, and doesn't let you use the thing that you bought.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-6">Just remember: your phone is not a smartphone, it's a computer.</p>
						<div
							class="my-16 grid min-h-80 place-items-center rounded-lg border border-dashed border-(--line-strong) bg-(--paper) p-6 text-center shadow-[0_1.2rem_2.4rem_var(--page-shadow)] lg:-ml-36 lg:w-[calc(100%+18rem)]"
						>
							<p class="type-subheading text-(--muted)">[line art of outline of cydia logo in background?]</p>
						</div>
						<p>Being able to use our devices as we choose, it'd be like having a bicycle for the mind.</p>
						<p class="mt-16">
							An open system is where a bicycle is yours to ride, repair, and share however you need – and
							computers are no different.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-16">Simply put, what we want is:</p>
						<ol class="mt-5 ml-6 grid gap-3 text-(--ink)">
							<li>
								<span class="font-semibold text-(--accent-strong)">1.</span>
								No forced identity. Developers shouldn't need government ID to publish software.
							</li>
							<li>
								<span class="font-semibold text-(--accent-strong)">2.</span>
								No artificial delays. Users shouldn't wait 24 hours (or ever) to install an app on a device they
								own.
							</li>
							<li>
								<span class="font-semibold text-(--accent-strong)">3.</span>
								Local safety. Scam protection should come from open on-device models – not a corporation deciding
								what you're allowed to do.
							</li>
						</ol>
						<p class="mt-16">We think these points are fair and achievable.</p>
						<p class="mt-5">What we're really asking for is the right to be curious about what we depend on – because curiosity, in the end, is the only security system that's ever actually worked.</p>
						<p class="mt-16">
							Above anything else, these decisions make it easier and safer for people to make things.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-5 pl-8 italic">(and no matter what tactic is tried, we will succeed.)</p>
